# DURABLE REPRO — a BARE `Guard[T]` parameter whose body MUTATES a field
# fails the C build with an implicit declaration of `gorget_guard_clone`. The
# CoW materialize pass emits a private-copy clone helper for the bare-param
# receiver (materializing before the mutation) but `gorget_guard_clone` is
# not a runtime symbol — nothing declares or defines it, so cc1 refuses.
#
# DISTINCT root from the pointer-peeling guard-field family retired by
# `guard_of` (`src/ir/lowering/exprs/shared.rs`, 2026-07-27): after the peel
# the FIELD ACCESS is correct, but the caller-visible clone step still runs
# and this cell breaks earlier — that is why the READ-ONLY twin (no mutation)
# builds and silently prints 0 instead of the guarded value while the
# MUTATING one hits an undefined symbol at link time.
#
# INTENDED (asserted below): the same as a guard LOCAL — the write reaches
# the guarded value and both the callee and the caller observe `11`. In the
# reference-grade shape a bare `Guard[T]` receiver is either move-consumed
# without a clone or refused by the checker (single-owner-by-design carve-out
# in the CoW spec, `docs/devbook/11-copy-on-write.md`); this fixture asserts
# the semantic outcome, agnostic to which of those two lands.
#
# ⚠ THE MUTATION IS THE DISCRIMINATOR — a read-only probe over the same shape
# builds fine and merely prints the wrong value; a mutating probe fails the
# build outright. Probing read-only mis-scopes the fix.
#
# `#[ignore]`d in `tests/integration.rs::sound_guard_bare_param_mutate_undefined_symbol`
# — un-ignore when the CoW materialize pass either declines to clone
# single-owner guards or the runtime provides the helper.
struct Inner:
    int fd

int seed():
    return 10

void inc(int &n):
    n = n + 1

# BARE Guard param that MUTATES — the cell that fails the C build.
void via_bare(Guard[Inner] g):
    inc(&g.fd)
    print(g.fd)

void main():
    Mutex[Inner] m = Mutex[Inner](Inner(seed()))
    Guard[Inner] g = m.lock()
    via_bare(g)
